ich it's named in the 1960s  halifax city council forcibly removed  its black residents and  raised their homes it would take 40  years for an apology to come in 2010 and  the museum was built a short time later  modeled after the baptist church that  was once the hub  of africville's residents it was a  cultural hub for the african nova  scotian community especially people of  africville  but today as a museum it's it serves to  welcome people into this space and and  see why this space was so important to  people of africville but this museum  like so many others across the country  has been shut to visitors since last  spring  but rather than wait for visitors to  return museums are reaching out in  new ways for the africville museum it  was a cookbook project  a collection of recipes contributed by  former africville residents  people from all over the city and the  country are buying copies  what is the thing that often brings us  together food  it was such a great challenge at the  museum in swift current  staff responded to the pandemic by  mounting an exhibition  with the public's help to document how  those in southwest saskatchewan  lived through the first lockdown this  exhibition is called  pandemic pastimes photos and stories  about  what life was like last spring the  closing of the skate park  the closing of the playgrounds and all  these artifacts will be archived by the  museum for  future researchers so a hundred years  from now  people are going to be able to actually  do an  exhibition and have local information  in west kelowna the west bank first  nation had just moved its museum to this  mall when the pandemic hit  its doors shut cora lee miller started  reaching a new audience  with a series of popular upbeat videos  from  inside the museum there's too much doom  and gloom on the news already at least  with this we can get people to forget  about their problems at least  just for a little bit now all three of  those museums were among  1200 i